#!/usr/bin/env bash
# commit-msg -- two gates: "Subtraction considered:" and "Population delta:".
#
# ── GATE 1: "Subtraction considered:" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHAT IT STOPS: adding without first asking whether removing would do. The subject is the missing
# QUESTION, not the ratio -- fixing a defect honestly often adds lines, and that is not a violation.
#
# THE RULE, inline, because this file ships and a maintainer reading it here should not need a
# second document: this project's founding doctrine is Saint-Exupery's -- perfection is reached
# not when there is nothing left to add but when there is nothing left to take away. The practical
# form is a question asked BEFORE building: would removing something solve this instead? The
# trigger is a new moving part -- a new file, a new guard, a new config key, a new rule -- not
# every commit. This file is the wiring for the half of that population git can see.
#
# MEASURED population this gate covers (2026-09-23, applied retroactively to the unreleased delta):
#     commits that add a tracked file   77 / 192  (40%)
#     tracked files added              150
#   Recount: git log origin/main..HEAD --diff-filter=A --format='%h' --name-only | grep -cE '^[a-f0-9]{7,}$'
#
# WHAT THIS GATE STRUCTURALLY CANNOT SEE -- written down rather than left silent:
#   This repository keeps its maintainer notes in a directory git is told to ignore, so tooling built
#   there never reaches the index and this hook never fires for it. The case that PRODUCED this
#   ruling was exactly there: a 166-line instrument was built to police a hand-written counter,
#   without anyone asking "what if the counter went away instead?" -- and the instrument itself then
#   proved the counter was redundant, because each entry already carried a unique commit sha. The
#   gate for that population is the maintainer's own review record, not this hook. Two populations,
#   two gates; not one gate leaking.
#
# WHY IT CHECKS PRESENCE AND NOT CONTENT: the ruling's author named the failure mode when choosing it
# -- a required line becomes a ritual once it is tedious. Checking content would breed a sentence
# written to pass. If these lines start coming back empty, that is a signal to revisit the ruling,
# not to tighten this hook.
#
# ── GATE 2: "Population delta:" (2026-09-24, review ledger V318) ──────────────────────────────────
#
# WHAT IT STOPS: changing WHICH FILES OR LINES A RULE LOOKS AT without recording how many findings
# that moved. Widening a veto or narrowing a file_pattern can only ever REMOVE findings, and a removed
# finding leaves no trace anywhere -- not in the detection gate (which scores only what the answer key
# names), not in CI, not in the diff. The change reads as a small edit to a regex.
#
# MEASURED, the case that produced it: one commit added an "examples" veto to
# security/config-file-secret and the message mentioned it as a subordinate clause. A three-armed
# comparison run later -- the old pack re-ided and loaded beside the new one, so one binary, one tree,
# one config, zero rebuilds -- put the real figure at 31 -> 5 -> 3 findings on one corpus tree. MINUS
# 26 for that veto, minus 2 for the lockfile and op:// arms. It was the largest population change of
# its batch and no document carried the number.
#
# HOW TO PRODUCE THE LINE, which is one run and not a study:
#     node scripts/measure/rule-population.mjs <pack>/<rule> [name-filter]
#     ./target/release/zzop analyze --config corpus/audit/<tree>/zzop.config.jsonc     # before/after
#
# WHAT IT WATCHES: any changed line under rules/dsl/ or in shared_fragments.json that is not one of
# five metadata keys. A NEGATIVE filter, and the positive list it replaced was wrong in two ways at
# once (review ledger V332, external review round 29).
#
# It listed the population FIELDS by name -- and MEASURED, `"absent"` is a multi-line array in 32 of
# 32 rules and `"patterns"` in 56 of 56, so adding one element to a veto array changes no line that
# carries the key. Widening `absent` IS the commonest veto widening in this pack set, and it was the
# single case the gate could never see. The list was also short: `line_pattern` (33 rules), `any`
# (25), `trigger` (56), `after`, `after_in_same_function`, `trigger_in_loop`, `require_call_kind`,
# `strip_string_literals`, `key_pattern`, `algorithm_pattern`, `attr_absent`, `entropy_min` and
# `skip_value_equals_name` all move a population and none were in it.
#
# So the question is inverted: which changed lines in a rule pack are NOT about population? FOUR keys
# -- `id`, `axis`, `severity`, `message`. Everything else fires, including the `{`/`}`/`],`
# of an element being added or removed, which is exactly the case the key list missed.
#
# THE COST, stated rather than found later: a pure reformat of a pack demands the line too, and so
# does a `label`-only edit -- `label` was the fifth key here until 2026-09-25 and the note beside
# POP_METADATA below is the owner of why it came off (labels are wired by name, so swapping two
# rewires which arm triggers which). This paragraph said FIVE, and exempted `label`, for five days
# after the regex stopped agreeing; the file that rejects a contributor was the one reading wrong.
#
# NOT native (Rust) analyses -- their populations are code rather than a field, and a text gate cannot
# see that edit. NOT vocabulary.* config keys, which are the user's to set.
#
# Presence, not content -- same reason gate 1 gives in its own words above.

set -euo pipefail

MSG_FILE="${1:?commit-msg: no message file argument}"
MARKER='Subtraction considered:'

# Nothing added means this gate has no subject (bug fixes, doc typos, refreshed numbers).
# `|| true` is load-bearing: an empty result is the NORMAL case here (most commits add nothing),
# and under `set -e` a non-zero exit on the assignment would kill this hook BEFORE the floor below
# can say so -- a mute exit 1 that rejects every commit while printing nothing. check-shell-mute-floor
# is the guard that names this class.
ADDED="$(git diff --cached --diff-filter=A --name-only || true)"

# Comment lines are not the message -- a template hint must not satisfy the gate.
# NOT `| grep -q`: under `set -o pipefail` a -q match exits early, SIGPIPEs the producer, and the
# pipeline then reports failure even though the marker WAS found -- on a message long enough that the
# producer has not finished. A small canary never sees it; check-shell-pipe-sigpipe does.
#
# Structured as a CONDITION rather than an early `exit 0`, because gate 2 below has its own subject
# and must still run on a commit that adds no file. The first draft kept the early exit and would
# have made the new gate invisible on exactly the commits it is for: a veto widening adds no file.
if [ -n "$ADDED" ] && ! grep -v '^#' "$MSG_FILE" | grep -F "$MARKER" > /dev/null; then
  COUNT="$(printf '%s\n' "$ADDED" | grep -c . || true)"
  {
    echo "commit-msg: this commit adds ${COUNT} tracked file(s) and the message has no '${MARKER}' line."
    echo
    printf '%s\n' "$ADDED" | sed 's/^/    + /'
    echo
    echo "  Write the subtraction you considered before you added. Two lines is enough:"
    echo
    echo "    Subtraction considered: <what removing would have looked like>"
    echo "      -> <what that would have cost>"
    echo "    Chose to add, because <reason>."
    echo
    echo "  \"Nothing to remove\" is a valid answer -- recording that the question was ASKED is the whole point."
    echo "  Why: perfection is when there is nothing left to take away -- so ask before you add."
  } >&2
  exit 1
fi

POP_MARKER='Population delta:'
# The keys a rule pack changes WITHOUT changing what matches. Everything else is population.
#
# `label` WAS on this list and came off on 2026-09-25. The reason written next to
# it -- "a label names an arm for reporting; it does not decide what matches" -- is false:
# crates/core/src/dsl/def/pattern_fields.rs says `trigger` and `after` name a `patterns[].label` by
# equality, so swapping two labels rewires which arm triggers which. Measured: a diff that swaps two
# labels, in a pack that puts `"label"` on its own line (sql.json, reliability.json, security.json --
# 101 such lines), produced ZERO hits here and the gate stayed silent. The same edit in a one-line
# `{ "pattern": ..., "label": ... }` pack produced 4 hits -- i.e. the PACK'S FORMATTING decided whether
# the gate could see a population change. An exemption whose reach depends on whitespace is not an
# exemption, it is a hole. Cost accepted: reformatting a pack now asks for a population line.
POP_METADATA='^[+-][[:space:]]*"(id|axis|severity|message)"[[:space:]]*:'

# Only ADDED/REMOVED lines (`-U0` plus the `^[+-]` filter), so a rule that merely MOVED in the file is
# not read as a population change. The `+++`/`---` file headers are dropped for the same reason.
# `|| true` is load-bearing here for the reason gate 1 documents above.
POP_HITS="$(git diff --cached -U0 -- 'rules/dsl/*.json' 'rules/dsl/**/*.json' 'crates/core/src/dsl/shared_fragments.json' 2> /dev/null \
  | grep -E '^[+-]' | grep -vE '^(\+\+\+|---)' | grep -vE "$POP_METADATA" || true)"

# shared_fragments.json carries no per-rule keys of its own: every line in it IS a population that
# some rule interpolates, so ANY change to it counts. Asked separately rather than folded into the
# regex above, because folding it in would read like an exception to a field list.
FRAG_HITS="$(git diff --cached --name-only -- 'crates/core/src/dsl/shared_fragments.json' || true)"

if [ -n "${POP_HITS}${FRAG_HITS}" ] && ! grep -v '^#' "$MSG_FILE" | grep -F "$POP_MARKER" > /dev/null; then
  {
    echo "commit-msg: this commit changes what a rule LOOKS AT, and the message has no '${POP_MARKER}' line."
    echo
    [ -n "$POP_HITS" ] && printf '%s\n' "$POP_HITS" | head -20 | sed 's/^/    /'
    [ -n "$FRAG_HITS" ] && echo "    shared_fragments.json -- every line in it is a population some rule interpolates"
    echo
    echo "  Widening a veto or narrowing a file_pattern can ONLY remove findings, and a removed finding"
    echo "  leaves no trace: not in the detection gate, not in CI, not in this diff. Measure it:"
    echo
    echo "    node scripts/measure/rule-population.mjs <pack>/<rule> [name-filter]"
    echo "    ./target/release/zzop analyze --config corpus/audit/<tree>/zzop.config.jsonc   # before/after"
    echo
    echo "    Population delta: <rule> on <tree>, <before> -> <after> findings."
    echo
    echo "  \"No corpus tree reaches it\" is a valid answer and a useful one: it says the change is"
    echo "  UNMEASURED, which is a different state from measured-at-zero."
  } >&2
  exit 1
fi

exit 0
